<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">661–714</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf was an Umayyad general and governor, the most powerful servant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> Abd al-Malik and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-walid-i">al-Walid I</a>. He besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 692, ending the caliphate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Ibn al-Zubayr</a>, and then governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and the east from 694 until his death in 714, crushing the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-ashath">Ibn al-Ash'ath</a>. He sponsored the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and reforms of coinage and administration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-hajjaj-ibn-yusuf.jpg" alt="al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf" width="320" height="150" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Cplakidas (uploader) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_al-Hajjaj_ibn_Yusuf.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
